Once we started

00:09:02.663 --> 00:09:04.640
looking at this
some time ago, we

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.420
realized that one is the apps
are actually using machine

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:09.950
learning on-device, right?

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.786
This isn't something
that's entirely new.

00:09:12.786 --> 00:09:14.660
We actually came across
a bunch of developers

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.354
who have been able to do this.

00:09:16.354 --> 00:09:18.020
However, there were
a lot of challenges.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.330
It was quite difficult, and that
was for a number of reasons.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:24.230
One is building the
model itself and the fact

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.090
that you needed a
lot of training data

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.330
and then you needed a lot
of not only training data,

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:32.110
but also the ability to create
a mobile-optimized model.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:33.860
That was quite challenging
for developers.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.370
We met some developers that
had built their own runtimes

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:40.130
on-device just to
execute TensorFlow

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:41.540
models because at
the time, there

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.330
weren't any available runtimes
like TensorFlow Lite, which

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:45.536
we have right now.

00:09:45.536 --> 00:09:46.910
So there were a
lot of challenges

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.000
when we looked out there that
made machine learning on apps

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.670
quite challenging and we wanted
to address a lot of those

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:53.180
initially.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.450
So, for example,
we came across apps

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:57.170
that, from a use
case perspective,

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.290
that did like music learning,
and so basically you

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:01.340
have an instrument.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:02.930
You're playing on
an analog guitar,

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.402
like a real actual
instrument, and the phone

00:10:05.402 --> 00:10:07.610
is recording what you're
playing and trying to assess

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:08.980
how well you're playing.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.730
Well, actually, a lot
of that is happening

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.146
through on-device
machine learning

00:10:12.146 --> 00:10:14.230
models that are tuned
to echo cancellation,

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:16.480
to the environment you're
in, to the specific learning

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:17.292
patterns you have.

00:10:17.292 --> 00:10:19.000
And then we talked to
specific developers

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.340
that do that, like
Yousician, for example.

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:24.400
It's an app that's
on Android and iOS.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.650
We came across
apps that help you

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:29.800
manage what you eating, and
counting your calorie intake.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.160
We came across fitness apps that
try to track your movements.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.590
Or apps that allow you
to scan in content.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.080
So, for example,
Evernote is using

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.500
machine learning to allow you to
take a picture of a whiteboard

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.550
and then it kind of
indexes what's in it,

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:46.310
and allow you to
search for it later on.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:50.410
So one thing we quickly learned
is that this stuff is useful.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.120
It's actually used
by a few developers,

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.240
but it's very challenging.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.030
It's way too challenging
for it to become common use.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.500
And so our goal was to-- and
we repeated this across kind

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.850
of launch of ML Kit--

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:03.444
was to kind of make
ML not so special.

00:11:03.444 --> 00:11:05.610
Right now, machine learning
is like a special thing.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.690
You needed lots of education.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:09.730
You need a lot of background.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.370
You need a lot of experience
in math and other things

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:13.660
like that to use it.

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.650
But we wanted to just be
actually just another toolkit

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.400
that a developer has
access to that they

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.561
can solve specific problems
with when they need them to.

00:11:21.561 --> 00:11:23.560
And so that was kind of
the goal and the mission

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.420
behind ML Kit from day one.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.720
So to summarize,
yeah, we looked at--

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.050
there were use cases for it.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:31.990
It was used in practice, but
it was way too challenging.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:35.430
And so we wanted to address
some of those things

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.900
with ML Kit features
that we have launched,

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.150
and some that we
are still launching

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:41.269
in the next few months.

00:11:41.269 --> 00:11:42.560
MARK MANDEL: It sounds awesome.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.460
I'm guessing I can probably
picture what this looks like.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.084
And Sachin, maybe
you can talk to this.

00:11:46.084 --> 00:11:49.300
So if I actually want to
use ML Kit as a developer,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.732
I assume these are like
baked into the Firebase SDKs.

00:11:51.732 --> 00:11:52.940
Is that how I would use them.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:53.950
What does this look like?

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:55.241
SACHIN KOTWANI: That's correct.

00:11:55.241 --> 00:11:58.170
So depending on what
capabilities you want to use,

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:01.022
they're modularized in
different Firebase SDKs.

00:12:01.022 --> 00:12:02.730
But you know, just
with a couple of lines

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:04.780
in your build.gradle
or your Podfile,

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:06.910
you can get this
capability added.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:09.790
I must confess here, you know,
when the team was building

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.040
ML Kit, I was really
looking forward

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.500
to the experience as a
developer-- as a hacker,

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.400
myself-- just to jump in
and start using the APIs.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:20.230
And as I said, you
know just you just

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.270
add a few lines to
your build.gradle file,

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:24.410
depending on what
APIs you want to use.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.100
And then once you have an
image, or a set of images,

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.549
you can start calling
those APIs on them.

00:12:29.549 --> 00:12:31.090
And you can have a
prototype working.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:34.450
You know, if it took me about
15 minutes to get working,

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.330
I would expect the average
listener of this podcast

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:38.960
to do it much faster.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.350
As Brahim was saying, we
wanted to make ML not so

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.210
special, and very easy to use.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.550
And that's what we aim
to do with ML Kit--

00:12:45.550 --> 00:12:47.064
very simple to use APIs.

00:12:47.064 --> 00:12:47.980
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:48.810
MARK MANDEL: That sounds great.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.710
I know you also
mentioned that this

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:54.040
could be run both on the
phone as well as in the cloud.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.890
Is there a difference in
capabilities between the two

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:58.660
offerings there, or
are they the same?

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:00.159
Brahim, I see you
nodding your head.

00:13:00.159 --> 00:13:01.600
You want to answer
that question?

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:01.840
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: Yeah.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:02.340
Yeah, sure.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.579
I mean, I think the
benefits of on-device--

00:13:05.579 --> 00:13:07.120
there's always
trade-offs, obviously,

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.160
like everything else in life.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.520
On-device, what you get
is the real-time aspect.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:15.130
You don't need to upload a
ton of data to the cloud,

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.420
obviously, so it is--

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.490
it can be a [INAUDIBLE]
sensitive approach

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:19.690
to machine learning.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:22.820
And also, it's actually
free of charge.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:26.080
It runs on-device, and so the
API itself-- the use of the API

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.400
itself-- is free
to the developer.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:29.740
So those are some big points.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.900
But of course, you
also get limitations.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.195
On the cloud, you
have the power of GCP.

00:13:34.195 --> 00:13:36.400
You have the power of TPUs.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:38.380
You have, essentially,
the ability

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.810
to run and execute
incredibly large models that

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:41.630
give you more.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.713
So, for example, if you
look at our image labeling

00:13:43.713 --> 00:13:47.080
API, which we have both a cloud
and an on-device variation

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:50.527
of it, the cloud variation
has over 10,000 labels,

00:13:50.527 --> 00:13:53.110
meaning they can tell you about
10,000 things about this image

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:54.190
that you've submitted.

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:56.830
The on-device one
has little over 400.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.920
So you can do-- what we've seen
developers do typically, is

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.170
do core classification
on-device,

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.340
where they essentially look
for interesting things.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:08.090
So let's say I am trying
to classify an image

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:09.670
and want to make
sure that I only

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.740
do the cloud call
if I see that there

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.430
is some food in the image.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.310
So maybe I will actually--
or maybe plants or flowers--

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.146
and so maybe I'll run
the on-device API,

00:14:18.146 --> 00:14:20.860
see if I get any keywords
that I'm looking for,

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.930
like flower, or plant,
or something like that,

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.810
and if I do then I'm
like, oh, OK, I actually

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:28.060
want to know specifically
what kind of flower this is.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.580
Then I'm going to call
the Google Cloud Platform

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:34.660
API to then get the more
fine-grained classification.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.550
And so we do things,
these pairings.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:39.660
We see developers do this
in a number of places,

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:41.110
and we actually
are also invested

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:44.510
in making sure these transitions
are even more seamless

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:47.500
and even in building use cases
and journeys or end-to-end

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:50.020
built to be what we call hybrid.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:52.480
We have a bunch of exciting
stuff coming up there

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:57.070
in the future, but we see this
as an increasingly common use

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.284
case, of relying on both
cloud and on-device.

00:14:59.284 --> 00:15:00.700
MELANIE WARRICK:
I mean I know you

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:04.900
mentioned that a lot of the APIs
are geared towards more visual.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:07.030
What are some functionalities
that-- or APIs--

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.240
that you would like to see, or
you're seeing more of a need

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.522
for from the different
people that you work with?

00:15:12.522 --> 00:15:14.480
SACHIN KOTWANI: You know
it's still early days,

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.355
so we're still trying
to gather that feedback

00:15:16.355 --> 00:15:18.400
and see what the requests are.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.492
But you can obviously
imagine that some

00:15:20.492 --> 00:15:22.450
of the things that we're
starting to talk about

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.940
are speech, video--

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:27.650
you know, it follows
closely after image.

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:29.050
But, again, it's early days.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.184
We would love to
hear from developers,

00:15:31.184 --> 00:15:32.600
from the listeners
of the podcast,

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:34.100
if they have any
suggestions on what

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:35.380
they would like to see next.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.792
For now, I'd also like to add--

00:15:37.792 --> 00:15:39.250
and as Brahim said,
this is an area

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:40.645
that I'm passionate about--

00:15:40.645 --> 00:15:44.410
that we do have the capabilities
for forecasting models.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.140
So if there are any specific
capabilities that can be around

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:48.940
in TensorFlow Lite
models and developers

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:50.780
would like to use those.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:52.790
They can deploy
them immediately.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.390
They can upload their
TensorFlow Lite models to ML Kit

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:58.000
and start using them
for on-device inference.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.630
And of course, there are
always the GCP capabilities

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:04.850
there for someone wants to run
a TensorFlow model in the cloud.

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.610
You can interact
with those, as well.

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:07.510
MELANIE WARRICK: Are
there any resources

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:09.690
you'd recommend,
especially around models

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:10.990
that maybe somebody has done--

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:12.550
custom models that
others have done

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:14.554
that are sharing them
and open-sourcing them?

00:16:14.554 --> 00:16:16.470
SACHIN KOTWANI: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:20.470
TensorFlow has a website called
TensorFlowHub, and that's

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.860
one of the things that they
do, is share snippets of code

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.950
or pre-bundled modules
that people can leverage

00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:28.810
to create their own models.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.510
And, you know, as for
completed models goes,

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.490
of course, the usual
suspects apply.

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:37.680
You can always look on GitHub or
Hackernews or any of, you know,

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:38.775
Medium posts.

00:16:38.775 --> 00:16:40.150
There's a pretty
active community

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:43.120
out there building
TensorFlow models.

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:46.490
Now, I'd like to call out
that what ML Kit supports

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.270
is TensorFlow Lite models.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:51.050
So you probably also want
to visit the TensorFlow Lite

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.500
website to see what
models are already

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:55.600
available in the
TensorFlow Lite format,

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:58.110
and how to go about converting
TensorFlow models TensorFlow

00:16:58.110 --> 00:16:58.750
Lite.

00:16:58.750 --> 00:16:59.860
MARK MANDEL: Just out
of curiosity as well,

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:01.480
I was reading that-- just
actually as we were sitting

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:02.140
here--

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:03.810
what's the difference
there between TensorFlow

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:05.140
and TensorFlow
Lite, just so people

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:06.490
have at least a broad
understanding of what

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:07.370
that difference is?

00:17:07.370 --> 00:17:09.369
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI:
TensorFlow Lite is obviously

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:10.630
born out of TensorFlow.

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.569
It's essentially an
extension of TensorFlow.

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:16.050
It is built with mobile in mind.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.670
And so effectively, the
runtime is much smaller.

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:21.640
It goes from over a meg
to about 200 kilobytes.

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:25.810
The storage uses flat buffers so
that the storage of the models

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:27.940
is far more efficient
and smaller.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:30.370
And it also has a more
limited set of ops

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:33.040
that have been optimized
for mobile devices.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:34.900
Over time, the team
is looking to expand

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.510
the number of operators and
also the number of capabilities

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:39.700
that it has beyond
just these operators,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.470
but we're very much
taking, kind of,

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.150
start with what's
most needed on mobile,

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:45.370
and then expand from there.

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:46.450
TF Lite comes--

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.280
TensorFlow Lite comes
with a set of tools

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.460
that allow you to convert a
model from TensorFlow to be

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.500
TensorFlow Lite compatible.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.810
That can also be
done in the cloud

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:58.270
through some of
ML Kit's services,

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:01.540
but it does come with an offline
tool that can do that as well.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:04.464
And the team is continuing to
release a bunch of cool stuff.

00:18:04.464 --> 00:18:06.130
So I'm really excited
about the roadmap,

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:09.400
too, around tools that
allow you to compress models

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.680
and, you know, things
like quantization

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.980
of models that allow you to
have vastly more efficient

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:16.900
mobile-centric models.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:19.260
So lots-- lots and
lots happening there.

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.820
But yeah, it is essentially
an extension of TensorFlow,

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:24.880
and it was built from the
ground up with performance

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:26.500
and mobile in mind.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:28.120
You know, I'm very
mobile-centric,

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.526
but the TensorFlow
Lite team actually is--

00:18:30.526 --> 00:18:32.484
is they care a lot about
things, like you know,

00:18:32.484 --> 00:18:33.776
it's available on Raspberry Pi.

00:18:33.776 --> 00:18:36.192
It's available for-- and you
know, they're working on IOT.

00:18:36.192 --> 00:18:38.620
They're working on kind of
tiny, tiny little sensors

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:39.950
that can run the models.

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:43.000
TensorFlow Lite is
kind of TensorFlow

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:44.820
beyond kind of server side.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.922
And so they cover a ton
of different platforms,

00:18:46.922 --> 00:18:48.130
and they're pretty versatile.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:48.430
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:50.096
And just so I'm a
hundred percent clear,

00:18:50.096 --> 00:18:52.660
as well, TensorFlow Lite models
can only be run on-device,

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.536
or can it be both
on-device and in the cloud?

00:18:54.536 --> 00:18:56.201
SACHIN KOTWANI: That's
a great question.

00:18:56.201 --> 00:18:57.580
I don't know if anyone's ever--

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:59.788
you know, we've never tried
running a TensorFlow Lite

00:18:59.788 --> 00:19:01.586
model in the cloud.

00:19:01.586 --> 00:19:03.710
MARK MANDEL: I have no idea
about machine learning,

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:05.512
so I just ask silly questions.

00:19:05.512 --> 00:19:07.720
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, you
could run it in the cloud.

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:08.629
It's a computer.

00:19:08.629 --> 00:19:10.420
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI:
You just wouldn't do it

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:12.190
because you know
the point of it is

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:13.780
that it is far more limited--

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:13.940
SACHIN KOTWANI: Yeah.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:15.460
You mean, you know,
you just wouldn't.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.710
MARK MANDEL: I guess my point
is, you would build this

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:19.285
specifically for
running on-device

00:19:19.285 --> 00:19:21.160
and that's its purpose,
so you would do that.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:21.420
OK.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:21.900
Got it.

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:22.450
SACHIN KOTWANI: Absolutely.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:24.189
It's-- yes, it's
optimized for mobile,

00:19:24.189 --> 00:19:25.730
and that's what you
would use it for.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:26.938
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, great.

00:19:26.938 --> 00:19:29.770
What other things have we
not covered around, you know,

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:32.260
ML Kit and how useful
it is for those

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.220
who are working on
Android or working in IOT

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:35.340
or looking at devices?

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:37.090
What are some of the
other tips and tricks

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:38.270
that you'd recommend?

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:39.050
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: I
have a couple of things

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:40.424
that I think are
quite important.

00:19:40.424 --> 00:19:42.210
One is that ML Kit
is cross-platform,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.307
so it's actually
iOS and Android.

00:19:44.307 --> 00:19:45.890
And this was really
important for us--

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:46.020
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.410
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: --as someone
who was on the Android team,

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:49.618
you know one of the first
things I asked my execs for

00:19:49.618 --> 00:19:51.740
is like, hey-- we're going
to need iOS engineers.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:53.740
And that's because when
we talked to developers,

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:56.002
they think of machine
learning as a thing.

00:19:56.002 --> 00:19:57.460
They don't think
of Android machine

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.560
learning and iOS
machine learning.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:01.300
And so it was very
important that we

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.250
offer a cross-platform
solution, and we

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.517
think that is actually one
of the most compelling,

00:20:05.517 --> 00:20:07.350
in addition to our
[INAUDIBLE] capabilities,

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.480
so that's a very unique
differentiator of ML Kit

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:11.830
that we're excited about.

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:12.887
That's number one.

00:20:12.887 --> 00:20:14.470
The other aspect I
wanted to highlight

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:17.420
is the fact that when we talk
about making ML Kit not so

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:21.560
special, one of the key
things about that was that--

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:24.470
hey, there are a bunch of like,
traditional mobile challenges

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:25.700
that developers care about.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:29.330
Things like binary
size, and A/B testing,

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.820
and all this other stuff
that had been essentially,

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:35.240
you know, not impossible
but really hard for ML.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.310
So today, if let's say, you
wanted to A/B test two models,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:43.304
you really had to bundle two
potentially 10 meg models each,

00:20:43.304 --> 00:20:44.720
and then you'd be
stuck with them.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.985
You bundle them to the
APK, you'll ship the APK,

00:20:46.985 --> 00:20:48.860
you'll do some offline
tests, and then you're

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:50.810
stuck with those two
models for the duration

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:51.680
of your experiments.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:55.444
You cannot really iterate any
faster than your APK release.

00:20:55.444 --> 00:20:57.110
And developers have
been trying to solve

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:58.530
these problems for a long time.

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:00.030
And they've been
solved for mobile--

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.960
those many, many platforms,
most notably Firebase, which

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:06.380
has things like remote config
and A/B testing and Google

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.310
Analytics.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.680
And so we wanted
to bring and solve

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:12.080
some of these like,
just mobile challenges

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.560
for machine learning.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.920
And so a lot of the custom
model stuff that we've built

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:19.310
is around model deployment--
out-of-band of an APK update,

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:22.122
or using a remote config
to actually name the model

00:21:22.122 --> 00:21:24.080
and then retrieve the
right model based on what

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.700
experiment group you're in.

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:29.210
So we made, essentially,
experimenting with ML models

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:32.271
literally a one-line
change in your app.

00:21:32.271 --> 00:21:33.770
And that's something
that, you know,

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:36.020
Sachin is really,
really invested in

00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:39.470
and pushing the envelope further
and further in that direction.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.100
But I just wanted
to reiterate that is

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:42.770
when we said we
want ML to be not so

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:45.929
special, we weren't just solving
machine learning problems.

00:21:45.929 --> 00:21:47.720
We weren't just thinking
about how to build

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:49.400
the best and greatest models.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:50.822
We were also
thinking about, hey,

00:21:50.822 --> 00:21:53.030
like, how do we solve stuff
that developers just care

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:54.799
about, like APK size, and so--

00:21:54.799 --> 00:21:55.340
I don't know.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:57.423
Sachin, you want to dig
into the compression stuff

00:21:57.423 --> 00:21:59.060
from an APK size perspective?

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:00.620
SACHIN KOTWANI: Yeah.

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:03.530
So you know, one of
the things that I

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:06.320
want to reiterate that
Brahim was saying first was--

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:08.790
ML Kit doesn't exist in
isolation within Firebase,

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:09.290
right?

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:10.710
There is a reason
why it's there.

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:14.810
It's because Firebase offers an
additional very, very broad set

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.880
of tools that are useful.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:19.700
And services that are useful
for mobile developers--

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:23.030
remote config, analytics,
and A/B testing

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:24.740
are just a few examples.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:27.050
Within the ML Kit
family itself, model

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:31.610
compression and conversion
at specific use case

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:33.000
for TensorFlow models.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.000
So let's say you want to
do image classification

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:36.791
and you have a model
that you have invested

00:22:36.791 --> 00:22:38.150
a lot of time and effort in.

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:40.580
Perhaps you have a
research team that

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:43.580
optimizes them to make
them work really well

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.080
and to increase the precision.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:47.900
And now you want to get
it working on mobile.

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:51.946
We have functionality that would
convert to TensorFlow Lite not

00:22:51.946 --> 00:22:54.320
only converted from TensorFlow
to TensorFlow Lite, right,

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.030
but also compress
it to make sure

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:59.690
that it runs efficiently and
doesn't take too much storage

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.630
space on the device.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.960
So that's one area that we
are still actively working on.

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:08.390
We are testing it with a
few partners right now.

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:12.740
We talked about Fishbrain
in other forums as well.

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:16.340
They took models that were
several hundred megs in size

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.590
and reduced them
by a factor of 10

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:20.902
without losing much
precision at all.

00:23:20.902 --> 00:23:22.860
And actually, I think in
their particular case,

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:24.949
we were able to even
increase it, which is great.

00:23:24.949 --> 00:23:26.740
So that's about
compression and conversion.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:29.690
Another area that I'm really
excited about-- and you folks

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:32.630
asked about typical
use cases, you know,

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:34.970
that our developers
and our early partners

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.150
would use ML Kit for.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:37.850
One area that I'm
really excited about

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.920
is about what developers come
up with because machine learning

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.190
is such a different paradigm
from any other software

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:43.820
development.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:45.361
You know, you can
download a library.

00:23:45.361 --> 00:23:46.700
You can add some UI.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.200
You can change a
database, right?

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:51.230
Those are common concepts that
are great and still important--

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:52.310
very much alive.

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:54.900
But machine learning is so
different from any of that,

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:57.830
and it can provide such
different capabilities.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.900
So that's one area
that I'm really

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:03.222
excited about is to see what
new things developers build

00:24:03.222 --> 00:24:04.430
when it comes to these tools.

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:05.270
MELANIE WARRICK:
Actually, just one thing

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:06.590
I want to make
sure I'm clear on.

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:08.464
When you're talking
about ML Kit and running,

00:24:08.464 --> 00:24:10.580
especially custom
models, all of this

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:12.350
is from the inference side.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:15.922
Is ML Kit also geared to help
people do training of models?

00:24:15.922 --> 00:24:18.380
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: The short
answer is, not at the moment.

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.950
There are lots of investments
around on-device learning.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:24.260
That's one area-- the one
area that we're looking at.

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:27.830
There are things on Federated
Learning where it's--

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:31.280
Google published a paper on
that about a year or two ago.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:34.880
For those who are curious,
a super, super cool paper to

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.530
read.

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:39.560
But, yeah-- nothing
in the immediate--

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:41.990
currently that allows us
to help with training.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:43.406
MELANIE WARRICK:
That is something

00:24:43.406 --> 00:24:46.220
you guys have in your mind as
what you're looking at, yeah?

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:48.220
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: It's
absolutely in our mind,

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:52.130
and we know that Google Cloud is
making investments in that area

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:54.380
as well through
auto-ML and others.

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:57.620
And so we're definitely
working across Google

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.550
to make that a
reality on ML Kit.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:01.550
MARK MANDEL: I've got to
ask the tough question.

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:04.219
This sounds great for mobile,
but Firebase supports the web.

00:25:04.219 --> 00:25:06.260
Are we going to get some
JavaScript APIs for some

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:07.665
of this stuff anytime soon?

00:25:07.665 --> 00:25:09.945
[LAUGHING]

00:25:09.945 --> 00:25:12.320
SACHIN KOTWANI: Of course,
the tough question goes to me.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:13.640
That's a great question.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:17.240
And I want to get that
question more and more

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:19.640
because the more we
get that question,

00:25:19.640 --> 00:25:23.120
the more it means that we should
be working in that aspect.

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:26.780
So, for now, as I said, it
works on Android and iOS.

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:28.490
We are hearing from
a few developers

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:30.380
that they would like
to see it in mobile

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.600
and I want to, as I
said, keep hearing that.

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:37.700
For now, if anyone wants to do
machine learning on the web,

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:39.890
I would point them
to TensorFlow.js.

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:41.930
There are obviously
a lot of models

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:44.540
that are open-source and
available and ready to use

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:47.660
in TensorFlow format, so
that's one way to go for now.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:48.560
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:50.351
Anything that you want
to talk about that's

00:25:50.351 --> 00:25:52.370
potentially exciting on
the roadmap for ML Kit

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:54.020
that people should
keep an eye out for?

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:54.440
SACHIN KOTWANI: Oh, man.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.160
There are so many
exciting things.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.370
And I don't know if I can
talk about any of them.

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:01.580
Brahim is looking at
me like, do not say.

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:04.270
[LAUGHING]

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:05.720
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: No, I mean--

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:07.730
I think some of the stuff that--

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:10.250
one is obviously reiterating
that what we have in the market

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:10.970
now.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:13.630
It's things like
image labeling APIs

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:15.500
is sometimes mind-boggling.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.690
You have an API that on-device
you give it an image,

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:18.773
it tells you what's in it.

00:26:18.773 --> 00:26:19.800
That is super cool.

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:20.570
MELANIE WARRICK: Definitely.

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:22.111
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI:
We also are super

00:26:22.111 --> 00:26:24.751
excited about the new face
contour API in a beta right

00:26:24.751 --> 00:26:25.250
now.

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:27.140
We're testing with
a few developers.

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:29.690
So that's going to be super
cool and if you see it--

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:31.740
you saw a demo of
Sachin doing this

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:34.742
at I/O. It's now a legendary
get food in the team.

00:26:34.742 --> 00:26:35.450
MARK MANDEL: Hmm.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:37.825
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: So that's
going to be super exciting.

00:26:37.825 --> 00:26:39.390
And just look for
us to keep pushing.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:42.270
You know the promise we made
to developers, quite publicly

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:45.500
at I/O, is that we will be
knocking on Google Research's

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:48.860
doors and asking them to
bring their capabilities

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:51.710
to developers in the
nice-to-use, easy-to-use

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:54.860
packaging, and we're continuing
to do that behind the scenes.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:59.640
We're continuing to push
for making Google's best

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:02.010
machine learning technologies
available through ML Kit

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:05.550
and that's the-- that's a
generic promise we're making.

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:08.990
In terms of specifics,
I think we'll

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:10.820
have to leave it at
what's coming now

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.259
as a white list, and
all that good stuff.

00:27:13.259 --> 00:27:15.050
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
thank you both again

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.900
for coming on the podcast
to talk to us about ML Kit.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:21.380
Just any additional
last-minute plugs or things

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:22.190
you're speaking at?

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:24.350
A research that you want
to let people know about?

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:24.890
SACHIN KOTWANI: Yeah.

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.960
You know, I would just
really encourage everyone

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:29.060
to give ML Kit a try.

00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:32.780
You know, at least from
the experience, and the fun

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:35.270
part of it and just getting
up and running really quick

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:36.890
and seeing results.

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:39.480
It's a very, very
rewarding experience.

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:41.606
So I'd love for all
of you to try it out

00:27:41.606 --> 00:27:42.980
and to let us know
what you think

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.750
and what you would
like to see next year.

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:48.920
We have quite a lot of
things that we have in mind

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:51.620
that we want to work
on and deliver soon,

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:54.260
but we also look forward
to hearing from all of you

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.150
to learn what things
you'd like to see next.

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:56.750
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:58.280
Well, thank you to
both of you for coming

00:27:58.280 --> 00:27:59.580
and hanging out with us today.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:27:59.780
BRAHIM ELBOUCHIKHI: All right.

